WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:01.000 --> 00:15.430
[Cameras shuttering.]

00:15.430 --> 00:22.290
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE: Good afternoon.

00:22.290 --> 00:25.870
Before we get to questions, I'll summarize today's policy action

00:25.870 --> 00:27.170
by the Federal Open Market Committee

00:27.170 --> 00:30.570
and then I'll place the Committee's policy decision in the context

00:30.570 --> 00:34.380
of our economic outlook and our collective judgment regarding the appropriate

00:34.380 --> 00:36.820
path of monetary policy.

00:36.820 --> 00:40.440
As indicated in the statement released earlier this afternoon,

00:40.440 --> 00:43.870
the Committee is maintaining the highly accommodative policies

00:43.870 --> 00:47.080
that we initiated at previous meetings.

00:47.080 --> 00:50.930
We decided to keep the target range for the federal funds rate at 0

00:50.930 --> 00:55.570
to ¼ percent, and we continue to anticipate that economic conditions are likely

00:55.570 --> 00:59.100
to warrant exceptionally low levels of the federal funds rate

00:59.100 --> 01:02.440
at least through late 2014.

01:02.440 --> 01:04.570
Our program to extend the average maturity

01:04.570 --> 01:06.870
of the Federal Reserve's security holdings-announced

01:06.870 --> 01:10.710
in September-will continue as scheduled.

01:10.710 --> 01:12.630
Each of these policy actions is intended

01:12.630 --> 01:16.990
to foster accommodative financial conditions that support the economic recovery

01:16.990 --> 01:19.350
in a context of price stability.

01:19.350 --> 01:24.750
In conjunction with today's meeting, FOMC participants-the 5 Board members

01:24.750 --> 01:28.500
and the 12 Reserve Bank presidents-submitted their individual economic

01:28.500 --> 01:32.020
projections and policy assessments for the years 2012

01:32.020 --> 01:35.840
to 2014 and over the long run.

01:35.840 --> 01:41.610
These projections serve as important inputs into the Committee's deliberations.

01:41.610 --> 01:45.530
Incoming information suggests that the economy has been expanding moderately.

01:45.530 --> 01:48.720
Most Committee participants expect economic growth to remain moderate

01:48.720 --> 01:52.530
over coming quarters and then to pick up gradually.

01:52.530 --> 01:55.930
Among other factors, and not withstanding some signs of improvement,

01:55.930 --> 01:59.090
the ongoing weakness of the housing sector still represents a headwind

01:59.090 --> 02:00.850
for recovery.

02:00.850 --> 02:04.800
Strains in global financial markets, though less pronounced, generally,

02:04.800 --> 02:10.670
than last fall, continue to pose significant risks to the outlook.

02:10.670 --> 02:13.060
Labor market conditions have improved in recent months,

02:13.060 --> 02:17.330
with the unemployment rate having fallen nearly a percentage point since August.

02:17.330 --> 02:22.010
However, at 8.2 percent, the unemployment rate remains elevated.

02:22.010 --> 02:23.700
Looking ahead, the Committee anticipates

02:23.700 --> 02:27.600
that the unemployment rate will decline gradually over the next several years,

02:27.600 --> 02:31.040
reflecting the moderate pace of economic growth.

02:31.040 --> 02:33.640
Specifically, participants' projections for the unemployment rate

02:33.640 --> 02:37.800
in the fourth quarter of this year have a central tendency of 7.8

02:37.800 --> 02:46.060
to 8.0 percent, declining to 6.7 to 7.4 percent in the fourth quarter of 2014.

02:46.060 --> 02:49.060
For comparison, participants' estimates of the longer-run normal rate

02:49.060 --> 02:55.500
of unemployment have a central tendency of 5.2 to 6.0 percent.

02:55.500 --> 02:59.910
Inflation has picked up somewhat, mainly reflecting higher gasoline prices.

02:59.910 --> 03:03.420
However, as has been the case for other recent swings in oil prices,

03:03.420 --> 03:07.180
the Committee expects that effect to be only temporary.

03:07.180 --> 03:10.200
Moreover, survey measures and financial market indicators continue

03:10.200 --> 03:14.180
to show stability in longer-term inflation expectations.

03:14.180 --> 03:17.530
Consequently, we anticipate that inflation will subsequently run at

03:17.530 --> 03:21.760
or below the Committee's longer-run goal of 2 percent.

03:21.760 --> 03:25.660
In particular, participants' projections of inflation have a central tendency

03:25.660 --> 03:33.730
of 1.9 to 2.0 percent for 2012 and 1.7 to 2.0 percent for 2014.

03:33.730 --> 03:36.940
The economic projections submitted by FOMC participants are conditioned

03:36.940 --> 03:41.810
on their individual assessments of the appropriate path of monetary policy.

03:41.810 --> 03:45.630
As you can see from the chart labeled "Appropriate Timing of Policy Firming,"

03:45.630 --> 03:49.210
Committee participants have a range of views about when the initial increase

03:49.210 --> 03:52.890
in the federal funds rate is likely to be appropriate.

03:52.890 --> 03:56.200
Following a careful discussion of those views at today's meeting,

03:56.200 --> 03:58.670
the FOMC maintained its collective judgment

03:58.670 --> 04:02.170
that economic conditions will likely warrant exceptionally low levels

04:02.170 --> 04:06.220
for the federal funds rate at least through late 2014.

04:06.220 --> 04:10.780
In particular, a highly accommodative stance of monetary policy is warranted

04:10.780 --> 04:14.470
in light of the persistence of the factors restraining the pace of recovery

04:14.470 --> 04:17.800
and the ongoing risks to the economic outlook.

04:17.800 --> 04:21.430
Finally, the Committee took no new decisions regarding the Federal Reserve's

04:21.430 --> 04:25.730
balance sheet today, but we remain prepared to adjust our securities holdings

04:25.730 --> 04:29.250
as appropriate to promote a stronger economic recovery

04:29.250 --> 04:31.770
in the context of price stability.

04:31.770 --> 04:32.130
Thank you.

04:32.130 --> 04:34.250
I'd be glad to take your questions.

04:34.250 --> 04:37.250
DARREN GERSH.

04:37.250 --> 04:43.110
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

04:43.110 --> 04:44.780
Darren Gersh, Nightly Business Report.

04:44.780 --> 04:48.000
Some of your critics-I'm sure you're not going to be surprised-think

04:48.000 --> 04:51.350
that you're still being too cautious, that unemployment is still high,

04:51.350 --> 04:54.470
the economy may be slowing, inflation is subdued,

04:54.470 --> 04:56.330
and I know you just talked about the balance sheet.

04:56.330 --> 05:00.350
But given that, is the Committee now any closer to QE3

05:00.350 --> 05:02.070
than it was at its last meeting?

05:02.070 --> 05:03.280
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

05:03.280 --> 05:08.370
Well, first, the Committee has certainly been bold and aggressive

05:08.370 --> 05:10.740
in terms of easing monetary policy.

05:10.740 --> 05:15.620
We've maintained the federal funds rate close to zero since late 2008.

05:15.620 --> 05:18.560
We've had two rounds of so-called quantitative easing.

05:18.560 --> 05:21.950
We've had a maturity extension program, which is ongoing.

05:21.950 --> 05:26.460
We have offered guidance about the federal funds rate

05:26.460 --> 05:29.580
that goes into at least late 2014.

05:29.580 --> 05:36.460
So we have been very accommodative, and we remain prepared to do more as needed

05:36.460 --> 05:38.560
to make sure that this recovery continues

05:38.560 --> 05:40.630
and that inflation stays close to target.

05:40.630 --> 05:45.410
So, in particular, we will continue to assess, you know,

05:45.410 --> 05:47.740
looking at the economic outlook, looking at the risks,

05:47.740 --> 05:51.910
whether or not unemployment is making sufficient progress towards its longer-run

05:51.910 --> 05:56.230
normal level, and whether inflation is remaining close to target.

05:56.230 --> 06:00.030
And, if appropriate, and depending also on assessment of the costs

06:00.030 --> 06:02.930
and risks of additional policy actions,

06:02.930 --> 06:08.230
we remain entirely prepared to take additional balance sheet actions

06:08.230 --> 06:11.070
if necessary to achieve our objectives.

06:11.070 --> 06:14.200
So those tools remain very much on the table,

06:14.200 --> 06:17.650
and we will not hesitate to use them should the economy require

06:17.650 --> 06:21.490
that additional support.

06:21.490 --> 06:24.510
JON HILSENRATH.

06:24.510 --> 06:27.420
Mr. Chairman, Jon Hilsenrath from the Wall Street Journal.

06:27.420 --> 06:29.900
The Fed has been forecasting for some time

06:29.900 --> 06:35.470
that inflation would fall to 2 percent or below.

06:35.470 --> 06:40.220
The latest measures of inflation suggest that the core PCE is at the higher end

06:40.220 --> 06:44.470
of that range and many other measures are above 2 percent.

06:44.470 --> 06:47.790
I noticed that in your forecast today that the upper end

06:47.790 --> 06:51.530
of your forecasts all the way through 2014 have increased.

06:51.530 --> 06:57.050
Do you see a risk that the disinflationary forces in the economy might not be

06:57.050 --> 07:00.380
as strong as the Fed had been projecting for some time?

07:00.380 --> 07:02.600
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

07:02.600 --> 07:04.220
Well, I would just say, first,

07:04.220 --> 07:10.650
that our projections still have inflation very close to our 2 percent target.

07:10.650 --> 07:13.760
As you point out, core inflation and some other measures

07:13.760 --> 07:18.030
of underlying inflation have been a little stronger than expected.

07:18.030 --> 07:22.730
But I would say first that some of the movement in the first quarter,

07:22.730 --> 07:24.080
for example, seems to have come

07:24.080 --> 07:28.190
from transitory sources like nonmarket components.

07:28.190 --> 07:32.710
And the fundamentals of inflation-in particular, inflation expectations;

07:32.710 --> 07:39.440
the amount of slack in the economy; the commodity price behavior,

07:39.440 --> 07:43.650
which has been relatively well-controlled in recent months-all

07:43.650 --> 07:46.860
of those things suggest that inflation is going to stay close to

07:46.860 --> 07:49.420
or perhaps a bit below our 2 percent target.

07:49.420 --> 07:55.410
Now, as I mentioned in the opening remarks and as we said in our briefing,

07:55.410 --> 07:59.150
the recent rise in gasoline prices has created a temporary bulge

07:59.150 --> 08:01.710
in headline inflation, in overall inflation,

08:01.710 --> 08:05.000
but we expect that to pass through the system and,

08:05.000 --> 08:08.030
assuming no new shocks in the oil sector,

08:08.030 --> 08:12.180
inflation ought to moderate to about 2 percent later this year.

08:12.180 --> 08:12.940
JON HILSENRATH.

08:12.940 --> 08:15.660
Why did the lower bound in those forecasts rise?

08:15.660 --> 08:17.080
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

08:17.080 --> 08:24.110
The lower bound rose-again, this represents 17 distinct views-but I would guess

08:24.110 --> 08:28.480
that the reason is that the data have come in a little bit firmer,

08:28.480 --> 08:31.350
core inflation has been a little stronger than was expected.

08:31.350 --> 08:34.450
But those differences are not particularly large.

08:38.320 --> 08:40.020
PEDRO DA COSTA.

08:40.020 --> 08:42.780
Mr. Chairman, Pedro da Costa from Reuters.

08:42.780 --> 08:47.690
So we know that the Committee foresees rates staying very low until late 2014.

08:47.690 --> 08:49.330
What is your personal view on the timing

08:49.330 --> 08:51.910
of the first rate hike, on the likely timing?

08:51.910 --> 08:55.010
And second, do you see the Committee as having an easing bias

08:55.010 --> 08:57.660
at the moment or is it neutral?

08:57.660 --> 08:58.680
Could it go either way?

08:58.680 --> 08:59.920
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

08:59.920 --> 09:04.690
Well, I'm very comfortable with the consensus view that we enunciated today,

09:04.690 --> 09:08.810
and I think that the Committee broadly is comfortable-a 9-to-1 vote

09:08.810 --> 09:11.420
in favor of this guidance.

09:11.420 --> 09:16.250
So again, that represents a very accommodative stance of policy.

09:16.250 --> 09:20.270
Our intention is to maintain a highly accommodative stance of policy

09:20.270 --> 09:24.160
for the foreseeable future, and we remain able

09:24.160 --> 09:26.340
and willing to take further action if necessary.

09:26.340 --> 09:31.750
At the same time, I think it is worth noting that the forward guidance

09:31.750 --> 09:35.970
on the federal funds rate is conditional on the data,

09:35.970 --> 09:39.430
and if the data were to come in much stronger than expected,

09:39.430 --> 09:41.820
we would adjust the guidance appropriately.

09:41.820 --> 09:47.520
So it's not unconditional; it does depend on how the outlook evolves.

09:47.520 --> 09:51.790
And again, should the outlook strengthen notably,

09:51.790 --> 09:53.900
then we would have to respond to that.

09:53.900 --> 09:54.260
PEDRO DA COSTA.

09:54.260 --> 09:57.010
How much more weakness would you need to see for QE3 to be in place?

09:57.010 --> 09:59.590
Would you need to see an actual recession take place?

09:59.590 --> 10:00.810
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

10:00.810 --> 10:03.660
The question is, you know, "How much more weakness do we need?"

10:03.660 --> 10:07.300
The Committee has to make those assessments,

10:07.300 --> 10:11.230
and we've been working to try to provide more-explicit guidance,

10:11.230 --> 10:16.020
quantitative guidance about our policy reaction function, but so far, you know,

10:16.020 --> 10:18.080
we haven't really done that.

10:18.080 --> 10:22.080
And I can only say qualitatively that the Committee will continue to look

10:22.080 --> 10:23.880
at the evolution of the outlook,

10:23.880 --> 10:27.700
try to assess whether unemployment is making sufficient progress towards our

10:27.700 --> 10:31.760
objectives, and, in particular, whether the recovery is still continuing.

10:31.760 --> 10:36.590
And we remain prepared to use balance sheet tools to support the recovery

10:36.590 --> 10:40.800
and to help make sure that unemployment continues its downward path towards

10:40.800 --> 10:45.100
longer-run normal levels.

10:45.100 --> 10:47.780
STEVE LIESMAN.

10:47.780 --> 10:50.480
Mr. Chairman, according to the latest forecast,

10:50.480 --> 10:58.120
10 members of the FOMC see a 1 percent or higher fed funds rate in 2014;

10:58.120 --> 11:02.360
7 of them see a 2 percent or higher fed funds rate.

11:02.360 --> 11:07.080
Under that-those conditions, how can the guidance in the statement,

11:07.080 --> 11:10.660
that you remain exceptionally low through late 2014, be justified?

11:10.660 --> 11:14.900
And is there a point at which the dissonance between the individual forecasts

11:14.900 --> 11:18.560
and the guidance get to a point where one or the other is no longer tenable?

11:18.560 --> 11:19.450
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

11:19.450 --> 11:24.370
Well, there's certainly a range of views, as you've noted,

11:24.370 --> 11:29.800
but these projections are inputs into a Committee process.

11:29.800 --> 11:33.620
And it's in the Committee meeting that we had yesterday

11:33.620 --> 11:39.470
and today where we debate not only the possible outcomes, but also the risks,

11:39.470 --> 11:43.990
the uncertainties, all the things that inform our collective judgment.

11:43.990 --> 11:49.030
And as I said, the Committee had no difficulty coming to a consensus

11:49.030 --> 11:52.670
that the guidance that we gave is still appropriate.

11:52.670 --> 11:56.850
Again, if there's a substantial change in the economic outlook

11:56.850 --> 12:01.700
in either direction, then the guidance would change appropriately.

12:01.700 --> 12:04.290
But for now, I think the Committee is comfortable

12:04.290 --> 12:06.610
with the consensus statement that we put out.

12:06.610 --> 12:07.270
STEVE LIESMAN.

12:07.270 --> 12:14.840
Do you worry about creating confusion in the market between the guidance

12:14.840 --> 12:16.350
and the individual forecasts?

12:16.350 --> 12:16.900
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

12:16.900 --> 12:21.580
Well, again, the individual projections are inputs to the Committee decision,

12:21.580 --> 12:25.910
so the Committee decision is the critical element in that respect.

12:25.910 --> 12:31.120
We are continuing to work to become more transparent,

12:31.120 --> 12:35.160
and we have a variety of things that we're looking at,

12:35.160 --> 12:37.100
so you'll have to stay tuned for that.

12:37.100 --> 12:41.100
But again, the Committee was quite comfortable with the consensus

12:41.100 --> 12:44.210
that we have reported today.

12:44.210 --> 12:49.410
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

12:49.410 --> 12:52.410
Unemployment is too high, and you said you expect it

12:52.410 --> 12:54.570
to remain too high for years to come.

12:54.570 --> 12:56.320
Inflation is under control, and you say

12:56.320 --> 12:58.690
that you expect it to remain under control.

12:58.690 --> 13:01.670
You say that you have additional tools available for you to use,

13:01.670 --> 13:03.470
but you're not using them right now.

13:03.470 --> 13:05.900
Under these circumstances, it's really hard for a lot of people

13:05.900 --> 13:09.300
to understand why you are not using those tools right now.

13:09.300 --> 13:10.160
Could you address that?

13:10.160 --> 13:15.350
And specifically, could you address whether your current views are inconsistent

13:15.350 --> 13:18.830
with the views on that subject that you held as an academic?

13:18.830 --> 13:19.480
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

13:19.480 --> 13:22.450
Yeah. Let me tackle that second part first.

13:22.450 --> 13:27.860
So there's this view circulating that the views I expressed about 15 years ago

13:27.860 --> 13:33.060
on the Bank of Japan are somehow inconsistent with our current policies.

13:33.060 --> 13:34.650
That is absolutely incorrect.

13:34.650 --> 13:38.560
Our-my views and our policies today are completely consistent

13:38.560 --> 13:40.820
with the views that I held at that time.

13:40.820 --> 13:44.150
I made two points at that time to the Bank of Japan.

13:44.150 --> 13:48.280
The first was that I believe that a determined central bank could

13:48.280 --> 13:53.340
and should work to eliminate deflation-that is, falling prices.

13:53.340 --> 13:59.080
The second point that I made was that when short-term interest rates hit zero,

13:59.080 --> 14:03.590
the tools of a central bank are no longer-are not exhausted,

14:03.590 --> 14:07.370
there are still other things that the central bank can do

14:07.370 --> 14:09.700
to create additional accommodation.

14:09.700 --> 14:14.010
Now, looking at the current situation in United States, we are not in deflation.

14:14.010 --> 14:17.200
When deflation became a significant risk in late 2010,

14:17.200 --> 14:21.380
or at least a modest risk in late 2010, we used additional balance sheet tools

14:21.380 --> 14:26.070
to help return inflation close to the 2 percent target.

14:26.070 --> 14:27.590
Likewise, we have been aggressive

14:27.590 --> 14:32.280
and creative in using non-federal-funds-rate-centered tools

14:32.280 --> 14:36.540
to achieve additional accommodation for the U.S. economy.

14:36.540 --> 14:41.760
So the very critical difference between the Japanese situation 15 years ago

14:41.760 --> 14:48.080
and the U.S. situation today is that Japan was in deflation, and, clearly,

14:48.080 --> 14:53.150
when you're in deflation and in recession, then both sides of your mandates,

14:53.150 --> 14:55.850
so to speak, are demanding additional accommodation.

14:55.850 --> 14:58.590
In this case, it's-we are not in deflation,

14:58.590 --> 15:01.870
we have an inflation rate that's close to our objective.

15:01.870 --> 15:03.460
Now, why don't we do more?

15:03.460 --> 15:06.660
Well, first I would again reiterate that we are doing a great deal;

15:06.660 --> 15:08.810
policy is extraordinarily accommodative.

15:08.810 --> 15:10.370
We-and I won't go through the list again,

15:10.370 --> 15:14.280
but you know all the things that we have done to try

15:14.280 --> 15:18.020
to provide support to the economy.

15:18.020 --> 15:21.950
I guess the question is, does it make sense

15:21.950 --> 15:25.460
to actively seek a higher inflation rate in order

15:25.460 --> 15:31.630
to achieve a slightly increased reduction-a slightly increased pace

15:31.630 --> 15:34.060
of reduction in the unemployment rate?

15:34.060 --> 15:39.730
The view of the Committee is that that would be very reckless.

15:39.730 --> 15:46.980
We have-we, the Federal Reserve, have spent 30 years building up credibility

15:46.980 --> 15:50.550
for low and stable inflation, which has proved extremely valuable

15:50.550 --> 15:55.800
in that we've been be able to take strong accommodative actions in the last four

15:55.800 --> 16:01.620
or five years to support the economy without leading to an unanchoring

16:01.620 --> 16:04.890
of inflation expectations or a destabilization of inflation.

16:04.890 --> 16:09.890
To risk that asset for what I think would be quite tentative

16:09.890 --> 16:14.700
and perhaps doubtful gains on the real side would be,

16:14.700 --> 16:18.830
I think, an unwise thing to do.

16:18.830 --> 16:20.600
KRISTINA PETERSON.

16:20.600 --> 16:25.480
Thank you.

16:25.480 --> 16:30.250
Given your warnings to lawmakers about the looming fiscal cliff,

16:30.250 --> 16:32.920
do you think the Fed has to take into account

16:32.920 --> 16:35.110
when Congress chooses to take action?

16:35.110 --> 16:38.520
If they waited 'til January, say, would you feel obligated to take

16:38.520 --> 16:42.080
into the-the potential economic blow into account?

16:42.080 --> 16:44.690
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

16:44.690 --> 16:47.750
Well, I think we'll have to take fiscal policy into account to some extent,

16:47.750 --> 16:53.820
but I think it's very important to say that if no action were to be taken

16:53.820 --> 16:57.880
by the fiscal authorities, the size of the fiscal cliff is such that there's,

16:57.880 --> 17:00.790
I think, absolutely no chance that the Federal Reserve could

17:00.790 --> 17:08.580
or would have any ability whatsoever to offset that effect on the economy.

17:08.580 --> 17:12.200
So I-as I have said many times before,

17:12.200 --> 17:19.090
it's imperative for Congress to give us a fiscal policy

17:19.090 --> 17:21.170
that achieves two principal objectives.

17:21.170 --> 17:26.110
The first is, of course, to achieve fiscal sustainability over the longer term;

17:26.110 --> 17:29.140
that is critical, and that's something that needs to be addressed.

17:29.140 --> 17:31.400
At the same time, I think that can be done in a way

17:31.400 --> 17:36.650
that doesn't endanger the short-term recovery of the economy.

17:36.650 --> 17:40.820
And I am concerned that if all the tax increases and spending cuts

17:40.820 --> 17:43.440
that are associated with the current law

17:43.440 --> 17:46.740
and which would take place-absent any congressional action-were to occur

17:46.740 --> 17:50.120
on January 1st, that that would be a significant risk to the recovery.

17:50.120 --> 17:54.300
So I'm looking and hoping that Congress will take actions

17:54.300 --> 18:04.190
that will address both sides of that-both requirements of a good fiscal policy.

18:04.190 --> 18:08.320
JOSH ZUMBRUN.

18:08.320 --> 18:10.510
Mr. Chairman, Josh Zumbrun from Bloomberg News.

18:10.510 --> 18:12.780
In today's statement you said, after coming quarters,

18:12.780 --> 18:15.330
you expect the economy "to pick up gradually."

18:15.330 --> 18:20.010
Yet in the forecast that we got today, we see the forecast for 2013 and 2014,

18:20.010 --> 18:23.310
the growth forecast, is downgraded.

18:23.310 --> 18:26.450
What caused you to downgrade your forecast for 2013

18:26.450 --> 18:30.350
and 2014 when you see a pickup after the coming quarters?

18:30.350 --> 18:31.100
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

18:31.100 --> 18:37.970
Well, again, these are the views of the participants, the 17 participants.

18:37.970 --> 18:43.550
So the basic feature that is described in our statement,

18:43.550 --> 18:49.060
which is that growth seems likely to pick up over time,

18:49.060 --> 18:51.470
is still obviously in our projections.

18:51.470 --> 18:55.110
The 2013 numbers are stronger than the 2012 numbers,

18:55.110 --> 18:58.810
and the 2014 numbers are stronger than the 2013 numbers.

18:58.810 --> 19:02.460
And the reason for that expected pickup over time is, first,

19:02.460 --> 19:04.220
a very accommodative monetary policy,

19:04.220 --> 19:07.660
which continues to provide support for the recovery.

19:07.660 --> 19:10.980
But in addition, some of the headwinds

19:10.980 --> 19:15.320
that have been affecting our recovery-such as the housing market,

19:15.320 --> 19:18.340
financial stresses, credit tightness,

19:18.340 --> 19:22.720
and so on-some of those things we hope will be lifting over time

19:22.720 --> 19:24.780
and will allow the economy to grow more quickly

19:24.780 --> 19:31.270
and approach more quickly its longer-run full employment level.

19:31.270 --> 19:36.420
The reason-I don't know precisely why there's been a slight downgrade

19:36.420 --> 19:41.970
in years further out, but I suspect that the fiscal issues may be part of that.

19:41.970 --> 19:47.240
ZACH GOLDFARB.

19:47.240 --> 19:50.470
Mr. Chairman, you've made the inflation target sound a little bit more

19:50.470 --> 19:55.610
like an inflation ceiling, and you suggested that a breaching of that ceiling

19:55.610 --> 19:58.230
or target would hurt the Fed's credibility.

19:58.230 --> 20:02.510
Can you explain a little bit more about why you think going 50 basis points

20:02.510 --> 20:06.570
or a full percentage point above the 2 percent target would hurt the Fed's

20:06.570 --> 20:08.540
credibility, and what that would do

20:08.540 --> 20:12.060
on the unemployment side if you were to do that?

20:12.060 --> 20:12.760
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

20:12.760 --> 20:15.710
So it's not a ceiling, it's a symmetric objective,

20:15.710 --> 20:19.650
and we attempt to bring inflation close to 2 percent.

20:19.650 --> 20:23.250
And in particular, if inflation were to jump

20:23.250 --> 20:26.070
for whatever reason-and we don't have, obviously don't have perfect control

20:26.070 --> 20:31.250
of inflation-we'll try to return inflation to 2 percent at a pace which takes

20:31.250 --> 20:34.400
into account the situation with respect to unemployment.

20:34.400 --> 20:41.180
The risk of higher inflation-you say 2½ percent; well,

20:41.180 --> 20:46.010
2½ percent expected change might involve a distribution of outcomes,

20:46.010 --> 20:49.020
some of which might be much higher than 2½ percent.

20:49.020 --> 20:54.890
And the concern we have is that if inflation were to run well above 2 percent

20:54.890 --> 21:01.220
for a protracted period, that the credibility

21:01.220 --> 21:05.370
and the well-anchored inflation expectations, which are such a valuable asset

21:05.370 --> 21:08.680
of the Federal Reserve, might become eroded,

21:08.680 --> 21:12.240
in which case we would in fact have less rather than more flexibility

21:12.240 --> 21:17.300
to use accommodative monetary policy to achieve our employment goals.

21:17.300 --> 21:22.380
I would cite to you, just as an example,

21:22.380 --> 21:26.930
if you look at Vice Chair Yellen's paper, which she gave-or speech,

21:26.930 --> 21:30.490
which she gave a couple of weeks ago, where she described a number of ways

21:30.490 --> 21:34.940
of looking at the late 2014 guidance.

21:34.940 --> 21:41.010
She showed there some so-called optimal policy rules that come from trying

21:41.010 --> 21:46.650
to get the best possible outcomes from our quantitative econometric models,

21:46.650 --> 21:50.520
and what you see, if you look at that, is that the best possible outcomes,

21:50.520 --> 21:54.430
assuming perfect certainty, assuming perfect foresight-very unrealistic

21:54.430 --> 21:58.370
assumptions-still involve inflation staying quite close to 2 percent.

21:58.370 --> 22:01.700
So there is no presumption even in our econometric models

22:01.700 --> 22:05.280
that you need inflation well above target in order

22:05.280 --> 22:07.070
to make progress on unemployment.

22:07.070 --> 22:08.810
PETER BARNES.

22:08.810 --> 22:13.400
Peter Barnes, Fox News, sir.

22:13.400 --> 22:18.030
For those of us who try to make a living out of parsing these statements noted

22:18.030 --> 22:22.040
that in the section on strains in global financial markets,

22:22.040 --> 22:24.440
the Committee said this time around,

22:24.440 --> 22:26.050
"Strains in global financial markets continue

22:26.050 --> 22:28.980
to pose significant downside risks to the economic outlook."

22:28.980 --> 22:32.690
But in January, the Committee said that the strains were easing.

22:32.690 --> 22:35.950
What are we to read into this apparent change?

22:35.950 --> 22:38.050
Are strains tightening now?

22:38.050 --> 22:41.250
And what is your assessment of the situation in Europe

22:41.250 --> 22:44.450
with the debt crisis-are authorities there adequately addressing the

22:44.450 --> 22:45.050
debt crisis?

22:45.050 --> 22:45.230
Thank you.

22:45.230 --> 22:45.640
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

22:45.640 --> 22:47.420
Well, it's just supposed to be a factual description

22:47.420 --> 22:49.610
of what's happening in the environment.

22:49.610 --> 22:55.390
In January, the financial markets had calmed considerably,

22:55.390 --> 22:59.220
reflecting a number of steps taken in Europe, including, notably,

22:59.220 --> 23:03.560
the two large long-term refinancing operations by the European Central Bank,

23:03.560 --> 23:07.390
which helped finance Europe's banks for up to three years.

23:07.390 --> 23:12.320
In recent weeks, we've seen more market stress arising from concerns

23:12.320 --> 23:16.430
about the fiscal positions of Spain and Italy, and, you know,

23:16.430 --> 23:19.840
we've seen more volatility in our own markets related to that.

23:19.840 --> 23:23.790
So we're simply taking note of the fact that a portion of the improvement

23:23.790 --> 23:28.970
that we saw late last year and early this year in European financial markets

23:28.970 --> 23:32.410
and in our own financial markets has been reversed recently,

23:32.410 --> 23:35.780
nothing more intended than that.

23:35.780 --> 23:39.840
As you know, I had the opportunity over the weekend to speak with many

23:39.840 --> 23:45.010
of my European and other international colleagues because we had the G-20

23:45.010 --> 23:48.520
and the IMF meetings here in Washington, and so we had plenty of opportunities

23:48.520 --> 23:51.930
to discuss the European situation.

23:51.930 --> 23:57.390
I think it's true that the Europeans have made substantial progress overall.

23:57.390 --> 24:06.230
I include not only the two LTRO operations, but also the Greek debt deal,

24:06.230 --> 24:08.570
the work on the fiscal compact,

24:08.570 --> 24:14.470
and recently the setting up of a larger financial firewall that could be used

24:14.470 --> 24:20.400
to avoid contagion should another country face serious financial distress.

24:20.400 --> 24:24.790
So progress has been made, but obviously, judging by market conditions,

24:24.790 --> 24:26.490
there's still more work to be done,

24:26.490 --> 24:32.340
and we are counting on our European colleagues to continue to follow

24:32.340 --> 24:36.310
through their commitments and to put very strong effort

24:36.310 --> 24:42.460
into addressing what remain significant problems and concerns in Europe.

24:42.460 --> 24:46.480
ROBIN HARDING.

24:46.480 --> 24:48.200
Robin Harding from the Financial Times.

24:48.200 --> 24:49.930
Mr. Chairman, two questions.

24:49.930 --> 24:53.640
First, to follow up on Steve's point about the interest rate forecast earlier:

24:53.640 --> 24:58.330
There are now only four FOMC participants who forecast

24:58.330 --> 25:02.770
that rates will still be close to zero at the end of 2014.

25:02.770 --> 25:06.040
Given that you said the Committee is entirely happy with that language

25:06.040 --> 25:10.130
in the statement, what information can we then take from the forecasts

25:10.130 --> 25:11.650
when they change like that?

25:11.650 --> 25:16.490
The second point-sorry-if you were to decide you wanted to do more

25:16.490 --> 25:19.700
to support the economy at some point, could you run us through the set

25:19.700 --> 25:21.470
of feasible options to do that?

25:21.470 --> 25:22.440
Thank you.

25:22.440 --> 25:23.790
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

25:23.790 --> 25:28.990
Sure. Well, if you compare this set of forecasts with January,

25:28.990 --> 25:32.550
you'll see that a few members have pulled in their expected date.

25:32.550 --> 25:39.120
But let me just reiterate that, once again,

25:39.120 --> 25:43.120
that these are just inputs into a decision process.

25:43.120 --> 25:48.020
They represent individual estimates, and, quite admittedly,

25:48.020 --> 25:50.770
as many of the participants said around the table, you know,

25:50.770 --> 25:54.610
these are uncertain from an individual point of view.

25:54.610 --> 25:59.080
And as we discussed the range of considerations,

25:59.080 --> 26:02.500
including the fact that we remain uncertain about how the economy is going

26:02.500 --> 26:06.300
to evolve, I've talked about, for example, the so-called Okun's law puzzle

26:06.300 --> 26:10.020
about whether or not unemployment will continue fall quickly

26:10.020 --> 26:12.190
or whether it will begin to level out.

26:12.190 --> 26:16.420
Given those uncertainties, given the risks like the European situation

26:16.420 --> 26:19.580
or the fiscal cliff that we've just discussed here,

26:19.580 --> 26:22.800
that there was a quite reasonable case

26:22.800 --> 26:28.230
for maintaining the guidance at late 2014.

26:28.230 --> 26:33.270
And as I said, there was a great deal of comfort among the participants

26:33.270 --> 26:38.740
and the Committee members with maintaining this guidance.

26:40.420 --> 26:42.370
GREG ROBB.

26:42.370 --> 26:44.930
Thank you.

26:44.930 --> 26:47.700
Greg Robb, MarketWatch.com.

26:47.700 --> 26:50.840
Judging from some of the speeches lately from Fed officials,

26:50.840 --> 26:55.420
it looks like too-big-to-fail is-they view this as the next step

26:55.420 --> 26:58.430
where attention should be paid and work should be done.

26:58.430 --> 27:03.650
There's even a proposal to have legislation to force bank divestitures.

27:03.650 --> 27:05.640
Could you talk about your views on these things?

27:05.640 --> 27:06.810
Thank you.

27:06.810 --> 27:07.210
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

27:07.210 --> 27:13.070
Yes. I believe that too-big-to-fail was an important cause,

27:13.070 --> 27:17.060
or at least a propagating mechanism, of the financial crisis.

27:17.060 --> 27:20.260
And I believe it's absolutely incumbent upon us to do all we can

27:20.260 --> 27:22.110
to eliminate too-big-to-fail.

27:22.110 --> 27:26.410
And what I mean by that is, a situation where a failing firm is bailed

27:26.410 --> 27:33.650
out because its collapse would have such adverse circumstance-consequences

27:33.650 --> 27:35.580
for the rest of the financial system.

27:35.580 --> 27:39.330
So we are working to get rid of too-big-to-fail,

27:39.330 --> 27:41.140
and I think making some progress.

27:41.140 --> 27:48.580
First of all, we are very substantially increasing the supervisory

27:48.580 --> 27:52.950
and regulatory oversight of large financial institutions; that includes,

27:52.950 --> 27:59.020
for example, the Basel III rules that require higher capital for all banks

27:59.020 --> 28:04.110
but particularly for systemically important banks, more liquidity requirements,

28:04.110 --> 28:09.650
stress tests, a range of tougher supervisory requirements that are-many

28:09.650 --> 28:14.290
of them are embedded in the rule we put out on 165-166,

28:14.290 --> 28:16.650
that section of the Dodd-Frank Act.

28:16.650 --> 28:19.480
So the first thing we're going to do is make sure

28:19.480 --> 28:22.450
that these large institutions are stronger,

28:22.450 --> 28:24.330
that it's much harder for them to fail,

28:24.330 --> 28:26.300
and that they are watched much more carefully

28:26.300 --> 28:29.330
and more actively by the supervisors.

28:29.330 --> 28:32.270
The other part of eliminating too-big-to-fail, though, is that,

28:32.270 --> 28:36.830
in that circumstance where a large complex financial firm does come to the brink

28:36.830 --> 28:41.320
of failure, that it must be allowed to fail; it must be safe to fail.

28:41.320 --> 28:44.990
And in that respect, one of the principal tools that we've gotten

28:44.990 --> 28:48.490
from Dodd-Frank is a so-called orderly liquidation authority,

28:48.490 --> 28:54.210
which is the FDIC's tool, and the Federal Reserve is working closely

28:54.210 --> 28:55.760
with the FDIC there.

28:55.760 --> 28:59.310
The idea is to apply the same kind of bank resolution tools

28:59.310 --> 29:02.650
that the FDIC has used for many years on domestic banks

29:02.650 --> 29:05.500
to large complex financial firms.

29:05.500 --> 29:09.260
Now, obviously, the complexity and the international aspects of these firms make

29:09.260 --> 29:13.910
that a much more challenging task, but I think we're making progress there.

29:13.910 --> 29:16.990
We have put out rules about so-called living wills,

29:16.990 --> 29:21.640
which will require large financial firms to essentially plan

29:21.640 --> 29:23.750
out how they would be disassembled.

29:23.750 --> 29:28.240
And we've continued to talk with international colleagues

29:28.240 --> 29:31.570
about how we would cooperate if a multinational firm had

29:31.570 --> 29:34.640
to be put into receivership.

29:34.640 --> 29:40.040
And, indeed, part of the reason that there's been some downgrade

29:40.040 --> 29:45.160
or potential downgrade for some U.S. financial institutions is the judgment

29:45.160 --> 29:49.970
of the ratings agencies that the so-called implicit government support

29:49.970 --> 29:53.370
that these institutions have is less than in the past.

29:53.370 --> 29:56.730
And so, I think, ultimately, what we'll need to have is a situation

29:56.730 --> 30:02.590
where large firms are both making judgments about their size

30:02.590 --> 30:06.700
and complexity based only on the economic benefits and costs

30:06.700 --> 30:11.670
and not on too-big-to-fail considerations, and that if there is failure,

30:11.670 --> 30:17.760
that it can be done and achieved without highly adverse consequences

30:17.760 --> 30:19.420
for the financial system and the economy.

30:19.420 --> 30:24.760
And that's our objective, and that's the way, I think, to end too-big-to-fail.

30:24.760 --> 30:27.150
CATHERINE HOLLANDER.

30:31.960 --> 30:35.260
You've been working hard to improve communications

30:35.260 --> 30:37.500
with both markets and the general public.

30:37.500 --> 30:39.040
What's your assessment of how both is done?

30:39.040 --> 30:41.350
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

30:41.350 --> 30:44.460
Well, I think that's part of your job to say.

30:44.460 --> 30:47.740
I think we're making progress.

30:47.740 --> 30:49.750
I think it was an important step

30:49.750 --> 30:55.630
to make clear what our inflation objective is-I thought that was important.

30:55.630 --> 31:00.880
And we put out a statement that described the overall policy approach

31:00.880 --> 31:02.430
of the Federal Reserve.

31:02.430 --> 31:06.370
We've taken a number of steps to improve communication,

31:06.370 --> 31:08.550
including these press conferences, of course.

31:08.550 --> 31:18.430
But also, by expanding our projections and by continued, you know,

31:18.430 --> 31:24.790
work doing press-doing,sorry, doing testimonies and speeches,

31:24.790 --> 31:26.310
and most recently as you know,

31:26.310 --> 31:31.290
I did some classes-I taught some classes at a local university.

31:31.290 --> 31:35.070
So we're doing our best both-to achieve, really, two objectives.

31:35.070 --> 31:38.060
One is to help make the Fed a little bit more understandable

31:38.060 --> 31:41.580
to the average person, because many people don't understand all the arcane

31:41.580 --> 31:44.390
aspects of the Federal Reserve and of monetary policy.

31:44.390 --> 31:48.890
And to be good voters and good citizens, they need to understand, you know,

31:48.890 --> 31:50.930
something about what the Fed does.

31:50.930 --> 31:55.590
And also, to communicate with markets so that markets can better appreciate,

31:55.590 --> 32:01.610
you know, what our monetary policy plans are in order so that interest rates

32:01.610 --> 32:05.050
and other asset prices can appropriately reflect those plans.

32:05.050 --> 32:06.680
I think the evidence on the latter point is

32:06.680 --> 32:11.770
that there is a better understanding of the Fed's policies.

32:11.770 --> 32:15.690
And, you know, we've seen, for example,

32:15.690 --> 32:19.720
less volatility in interest rates related to greater certainty

32:19.720 --> 32:21.720
about what the Fed is likely to do.

32:21.720 --> 32:26.070
But I want to emphasize that this is an ongoing task, it's a work in progress,

32:26.070 --> 32:27.910
and we're going to continue to look for ways

32:27.910 --> 32:32.250
to make ourselves better understand-more understandable

32:32.250 --> 32:35.300
and more transparent to the public.

32:36.550 --> 32:44.440
GREG IP. Greg Ip of the Economist.

32:44.440 --> 32:48.600
First, assume that you are not constrained by the zero nominal bound right now.

32:48.600 --> 32:51.540
What would the federal funds rate be, even if it were a negative number?

32:51.540 --> 32:54.350
And do you believe that you are at an equivalent degree

32:54.350 --> 32:57.540
of monetary accommodation today using your unconventional tools?

32:57.540 --> 33:01.610
And also, a quick supplemental-could you put some numbers on what-on the meaning

33:01.610 --> 33:03.400
of "exceptionally low" federal funds rate?

33:03.400 --> 33:05.780
For example, would a 1 percent federal funds rate qualify

33:05.780 --> 33:09.950
as "exceptionally low" at the end of 2014?

33:09.950 --> 33:11.120
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

33:11.120 --> 33:15.310
Well, the exact reading for the federal funds rate today,

33:15.310 --> 33:16.650
in the absence of the zero lower bound,

33:16.650 --> 33:19.880
would depend a lot on which particular rule, what particular model that you use.

33:19.880 --> 33:25.180
I don't want to cite a particular number, but it probably would be negative.

33:25.180 --> 33:28.930
And in that respect, we are trying to compensate for that by the use

33:28.930 --> 33:32.050
of nonstandard tools, including, as you know,

33:32.050 --> 33:35.320
almost a $3 trillion balance sheet.

33:36.590 --> 33:43.110
We see monetary policy as being approximately in the right place at this point,

33:43.110 --> 33:49.370
based on the analysis that we've been doing in-of the economy and the outlook.

33:49.370 --> 33:51.140
That doesn't mean we might not take further action;

33:51.140 --> 33:53.380
we are certainly prepared to take further action.

33:53.380 --> 33:57.830
But for the time being, it appears that we are more or less in the right place.

33:57.830 --> 34:00.530
GREG IP. "Exceptionally low"?

34:00.530 --> 34:01.390
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

34:01.390 --> 34:06.200
"Exceptionally low"; you know, one of the reasons that language

34:06.200 --> 34:11.510
in the statement is sometimes a little vaguer than you would like is

34:11.510 --> 34:17.030
because we are trying to get a consensus among 17 or at least 10 people,

34:17.030 --> 34:18.790
and different members or participants

34:18.790 --> 34:20.820
in the FOMC might have somewhat different views

34:20.820 --> 34:22.590
of what "exceptionally low" means.

34:22.590 --> 34:29.490
Personally, I think it means something close to where we are-where we are now.

34:29.490 --> 34:32.230
DONNA BORAK.

34:32.230 --> 34:34.530
Chairman, Donna Borak with American Banker.

34:34.530 --> 34:38.820
To follow up on the too-big-to-fail question, there has been acute discussion

34:38.820 --> 34:42.520
in recent months about calling for breaking up the banks even louder

34:42.520 --> 34:45.370
than before, even including some Fed officials.

34:45.370 --> 34:47.880
And you just talked about the need to eliminate too-big-to-fail.

34:47.880 --> 34:51.180
Of course, Dodd-Frank-that's the intent of it.

34:51.180 --> 34:54.030
My question is twofold: Do you agree?

34:54.030 --> 34:57.290
And is there an argument to be made for breaking up the banks?

34:57.290 --> 34:59.350
And is that something that the Fed should consider

34:59.350 --> 35:02.800
when weighing the importance of financial stability?

35:02.800 --> 35:05.660
And, sort of, to this earlier point that you made is,

35:05.660 --> 35:08.960
what in your view is the litmus test that will allow us to know

35:08.960 --> 35:13.840
that we can achieve ending too-big-to-fail through Dodd-Frank?

35:13.840 --> 35:14.680
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

35:14.680 --> 35:17.550
Well, there may be circumstances where a bank

35:17.550 --> 35:23.210
or financial institution is artificially large.

35:23.210 --> 35:30.140
And in that context, if it were posing a severe threat to financial stability,

35:30.140 --> 35:35.390
then I suppose that considering breaking it up would be something

35:35.390 --> 35:37.700
that regulators should look at.

35:37.700 --> 35:41.980
My own view, though, is that a more market-responsive way

35:41.980 --> 35:47.090
to address this problem is to eliminate the incentive to be too-big-to-fail.

35:47.090 --> 35:52.920
That is, to-through tougher supervisory oversight,

35:52.920 --> 35:56.690
through higher capital requirements, through greater liquidity requirements,

35:56.690 --> 36:03.270
through restrictions on interconnectedness, et cetera, take away the benefits

36:03.270 --> 36:08.380
or force firms to internalize the costs of being large and complex.

36:08.380 --> 36:11.530
And on the other side, as I was saying earlier,

36:11.530 --> 36:20.420
if we can safely unwind a failing firm, then we no longer have too-big-to-fail,

36:20.420 --> 36:24.480
obviously, and so I think that's a very, very important objective.

36:24.480 --> 36:30.390
The test would be that the financial markets that lend

36:30.390 --> 36:38.610
to large firms base their bond spreads and what they're willing to pay

36:38.610 --> 36:45.570
for the stock of those firms solely on the risk-taking and on the business model

36:45.570 --> 36:48.120
of those firms and not on the fact

36:48.120 --> 36:50.470
that there's some anticipation of a government bailout.

36:50.470 --> 36:55.680
And so I think market indicators will help us see our progress towards

36:55.680 --> 36:57.640
ending too-big-to-fail.

36:59.070 --> 37:04.450
STEVE BECKNER.

37:04.450 --> 37:07.450
Steve Beckner of Market News International.

37:07.450 --> 37:09.200
Mr. Chairman, good to see you.

37:09.200 --> 37:14.340
There's been some concern in markets about what will happen to bond yields

37:14.340 --> 37:19.450
at the expiration of Operation Twist on June 30th and some speculation

37:19.450 --> 37:25.960
on what the Fed might do-might need to do to keep downward pressure on yields.

37:25.960 --> 37:28.370
Is that a concern that you share?

37:28.370 --> 37:31.950
And do you and your colleagues feel the need not

37:31.950 --> 37:37.650
to disappoint these kinds of market expectations?

37:37.650 --> 37:38.450
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

37:38.450 --> 37:43.100
Well, to your last point, the purpose of monetary policy is

37:43.100 --> 37:46.720
to achieve our objectives of maximum employment and price stability,

37:46.720 --> 37:51.250
it's not to disappoint or not disappoint investors.

37:51.250 --> 37:54.560
So we will take actions based on those economic objectives

37:54.560 --> 37:58.600
and not try to achieve certain market outcomes.

37:58.600 --> 38:05.780
There's some disagreement, I think, about exactly how balance sheet actions

38:05.780 --> 38:10.810
by the Federal Reserve affect Treasury yields and other asset prices.

38:10.810 --> 38:13.580
The view that we have generally taken at the Fed,

38:13.580 --> 38:16.790
in which I think-for which I think the evidence is pretty good,

38:16.790 --> 38:23.240
is that it's the quantity of securities held by the Fed at a given time rather

38:23.240 --> 38:24.780
than the new purchases-the flow

38:24.780 --> 38:29.280
of new purchases-which is the primary determinant of interest rates.

38:29.280 --> 38:36.150
And if that theory is correct, then at such time that our purchases come

38:36.150 --> 38:39.980
to an end, there should be relatively minimal effects

38:39.980 --> 38:41.720
on interest rates at that time.

38:41.720 --> 38:46.260
And that, in fact, has been our-generally our experience in previous episodes

38:46.260 --> 38:51.360
where we have launched a program and then allowed it to come to a natural end.

38:51.360 --> 38:53.620
Of course, we'll continue to monitor the situation,

38:53.620 --> 38:59.120
and if we believe that financial conditions for whatever reason are inconsistent

38:59.120 --> 39:04.790
with our macroeconomic objectives, then we will act to fix that.

39:04.790 --> 39:13.430
But, again, our expectation is that, at whatever point that the purchases end,

39:13.430 --> 39:18.580
that financial markets being quite forward looking will have anticipated that,

39:18.580 --> 39:21.780
and that the effects ought to be moderate.

39:22.880 --> 39:25.500
AKIO FUJII.

39:25.500 --> 39:30.480
Thank you, Chairman.

39:32.070 --> 39:37.700
I am Akio Fujii from Nikkei newspaper.

39:37.700 --> 39:43.250
My question is follow-up for your comment about Japan and U.S. Now,

39:43.250 --> 39:50.250
are you confident U.S. economy would avoid long-term stagnation like we had

39:50.250 --> 39:53.600
in Japan, or so-called Japanization?

39:53.600 --> 40:00.020
And if it's the case, only policy response make a difference of Japan and U.S.,

40:00.020 --> 40:03.230
or are there any other factor make difference?

40:03.230 --> 40:04.020
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

40:04.020 --> 40:06.500
The last part again-are there measures?

40:06.500 --> 40:07.320
AKIO FUJII.

40:07.320 --> 40:11.190
Yes, policy response is the only reason make a difference U.S. and Japan

40:11.190 --> 40:16.010
or are there any other factor make a difference of U.S. and Japan situation?

40:16.010 --> 40:17.500
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

40:17.500 --> 40:23.260
Well, I think the-I would draw two distinctions between the U.S. and Japan,

40:23.260 --> 40:26.250
or the Japanese experience.

40:26.250 --> 40:30.650
The first is that, as I mentioned earlier-and I think this is very important-is

40:30.650 --> 40:35.610
that we acted aggressively and preemptively to avoid deflation.

40:35.610 --> 40:40.190
Now, of course, Japan had a much bigger bubble and a much bigger shock

40:40.190 --> 40:43.200
when the bubble collapsed, and so these differences may be

40:43.200 --> 40:44.790
certainly understandable.

40:44.790 --> 40:48.170
But, again, we did avoid deflation.

40:48.170 --> 40:52.110
The other thing which I think we have done reasonably well here

40:52.110 --> 40:57.000
in the United States was that we moved fairly quickly to make sure

40:57.000 --> 41:01.920
that our banks were recapitalized and were recognizing their bad assets.

41:01.920 --> 41:10.260
And I think the stress tests that we conducted last month are good evidence

41:10.260 --> 41:15.900
that the U.S. banking system is considerably stronger and, indeed,

41:15.900 --> 41:19.560
much more resilient than it was a couple of years ago.

41:19.560 --> 41:24.670
So those two things are positives and would tend to suggest

41:24.670 --> 41:29.430
that we will avoid some of the problems that Japan has faced.

41:29.430 --> 41:36.250
That being said, I think it's always better to be humble and just to,

41:36.250 --> 41:41.000
you know, avoid being too confident.

41:41.000 --> 41:45.910
And we need to continue to maintain a strong monetary policy support

41:45.910 --> 41:48.800
to make sure that the economy continues on a recovery path

41:48.800 --> 41:52.350
and returns to a more normal situation.

41:53.440 --> 41:59.220
SCOTT SPOERRY.

42:02.370 --> 42:06.370
Scott Spoerry with CNNMoney, sir.

42:06.370 --> 42:10.150
I wanted to ask you about the labor force participation rate.

42:10.150 --> 42:13.370
It's at the lowest level since the early '80s.

42:13.370 --> 42:16.920
Can you talk about why people are dropping out of the job market,

42:16.920 --> 42:19.890
whether it's a permanent structural problem,

42:19.890 --> 42:22.850
and what convinces you that things may change?

42:22.850 --> 42:25.290
If I could just a pop a second question to follow up on Steve

42:25.290 --> 42:28.990
and the bonds-your colleague Sheila Bair wrote this week

42:28.990 --> 42:34.990
that her-about her concern over being in a bond bubble.

42:34.990 --> 42:37.170
She said you should declare victory,

42:37.170 --> 42:39.170
and you should do something about puncturing

42:39.170 --> 42:41.230
that bubble before it gets too much out of control.

42:41.230 --> 42:43.030
Can you respond to that too?

42:43.030 --> 42:45.500
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

42:45.500 --> 42:51.290
On declaring victory, I think it's a little premature to declare victory.

42:51.290 --> 42:57.530
I think that keeping interest rates low is still appropriate for our economy.

42:57.530 --> 43:03.050
As for the bond bubble, interest rates are low for a lot of reasons;

43:03.050 --> 43:07.000
they include monetary policy, of course, but they also include a weak economy,

43:07.000 --> 43:11.750
low inflation expectations, and safe-haven demands for U.S. Treasuries.

43:11.750 --> 43:15.870
So, of course, interest rates will rise at some point.

43:15.870 --> 43:19.210
We hope that they do because that would be an indication

43:19.210 --> 43:21.570
that the economy is recovering and strengthening.

43:21.570 --> 43:27.140
And I think it's important for holders of long-term securities

43:27.140 --> 43:30.170
to manage their risks and pay attention to that.

43:30.170 --> 43:34.560
But all that being said, I think, again, that there are both good reasons

43:34.560 --> 43:39.860
to continue monetary ease and good reasons for interest rates to be low.

43:39.860 --> 43:41.980
The first part of your question was- SCOTT SPOERRY.

43:41.980 --> 43:42.860
About the- CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

43:42.860 --> 43:45.340
Oh, participation, labor participation, yes.

43:45.340 --> 43:49.190
Actually, we had a very good discussion of that at the meeting

43:49.190 --> 43:53.290
because it is an important issue, trying to assess how much of the change

43:53.290 --> 43:59.330
in employment is structural, how much is cyclical, and so on.

43:59.330 --> 44:05.360
There is, in fact, a downward trend in labor participation in the United States.

44:05.360 --> 44:07.810
It comes from, first, the fact

44:07.810 --> 44:11.080
that we're no longer getting increased participation from women;

44:11.080 --> 44:13.610
female participation has leveled out.

44:13.610 --> 44:20.110
And, secondly, because, as the society ages, and also for other reasons,

44:20.110 --> 44:23.060
male participation has been declining over time.

44:23.060 --> 44:26.510
So there is a downward trend that we have to take into account.

44:26.510 --> 44:34.530
The participants at the meeting, at least some of them, suggested, though,

44:34.530 --> 44:39.500
that a good bit of the decline in participation that we've seen

44:39.500 --> 44:43.720
in the last few years does represent cyclical factors.

44:43.720 --> 44:46.320
Much of it is young people, for example,

44:46.320 --> 44:51.000
who presumably are not out of the labor force indefinitely, but,

44:51.000 --> 44:55.110
given the weak job market, they are going to school

44:55.110 --> 44:58.210
or doing something else rather than working.

44:58.210 --> 45:03.780
So one possibility and one reason why the unemployment rate may not fall

45:03.780 --> 45:07.590
as quickly going forward is that as the economy strengthens,

45:07.590 --> 45:11.380
as the labor market strengthens, many of these folks are going to come back

45:11.380 --> 45:16.940
in the labor force looking for work, which I think would be a good thing.

45:16.940 --> 45:19.380
But we'll just have to see.

45:19.380 --> 45:24.490
But I do think-I think I would agree with the argument that a significant part

45:24.490 --> 45:27.990
of the decline over and above the downward trend

45:27.990 --> 45:32.900
in the participation rate is reflecting cyclical factors and should reverse

45:32.900 --> 45:35.840
when the economy gets stronger.

45:35.840 --> 45:43.480
DON LEE. Don Lee from the L.A. Times.

45:43.480 --> 45:48.440
Can you give us your assessment of the impact of the mild winter weather

45:48.440 --> 45:51.520
on job growth and what that might mean going forward?

45:51.520 --> 45:55.890
And related to that, I think you said last month that some

45:55.890 --> 46:01.270
of the recent job gains reflected a catch-up

46:01.270 --> 46:04.900
from outsize cuts right after the recession.

46:04.900 --> 46:09.010
I mean, given those two things, what kind of job growth can we expect?

46:09.010 --> 46:11.170
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

46:11.170 --> 46:16.380
Well, the weather issue just reflects how difficult it can be

46:16.380 --> 46:22.700
to make real-time assessments of the state of the economy.

46:22.700 --> 46:26.400
The weather has probably affected a number of things.

46:26.400 --> 46:34.190
It probably brought forward some of the hiring so that it made perhaps January

46:34.190 --> 46:36.310
and February artificially strong

46:36.310 --> 46:38.800
and March perhaps artificially a little bit weak.

46:38.800 --> 46:43.490
It probably affected construction, manufacturing.

46:43.490 --> 46:46.510
There's a number of things that were probably affected to some extent

46:46.510 --> 46:51.010
by the unusually warm weather that we had this winter,

46:51.010 --> 46:56.380
and we're doing our best to try to adjust for that.

46:56.380 --> 46:59.780
We're also looking at some of the seasonality issues that have arisen

46:59.780 --> 47:07.440
because of the unusually large recession in 2008 and 2009.

47:07.440 --> 47:12.220
So, again, that makes the data harder to interpret.

47:12.220 --> 47:20.070
I advanced the hypothesis that the increase in employment we've seen

47:20.070 --> 47:25.200
in the last five, six months might to some extent be greater

47:25.200 --> 47:29.700
than we'd expect going forward because it represented a one-time catch-up,

47:29.700 --> 47:35.370
undoing the very sharp layoffs in 2008 and 2009.

47:35.370 --> 47:37.010
That's only a hypothesis.

47:37.010 --> 47:38.980
We'll have to see going forward.

47:38.980 --> 47:45.070
If it's correct, then job gains will be somewhat less than the 250,000 a month

47:45.070 --> 47:47.310
that we've been seeing recently.

47:47.310 --> 47:50.920
But we don't know yet, we don't know yet.

47:50.920 --> 47:54.030
And I wouldn't draw too much conclusion from the March report.

47:54.030 --> 47:56.860
I think, again, because of weather and other factors,

47:56.860 --> 48:02.680
we really can't take a conclusive result from that.

48:02.680 --> 48:04.520
So, we'll continue to be watching the labor market.

48:04.520 --> 48:07.510
That's a very important consideration.

48:07.510 --> 48:10.630
If unemployment looks like it's no longer making progress,

48:10.630 --> 48:13.590
that'll be an important consideration in thinking about policy options.

48:13.590 --> 48:16.600
DON LEE. What kind of job growth, on average,

48:16.600 --> 48:19.710
is consistent with the unemployment projections that you've made?

48:19.710 --> 48:22.800
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

48:22.800 --> 48:26.650
Well, we need something-estimates differ.

48:26.650 --> 48:31.780
We need fewer jobs monthly to keep unemployment consistent

48:31.780 --> 48:37.750
or stable than in the past-I suppose more like 100,000 a month for stability.

48:37.750 --> 48:44.220
I don't have an exact answer, but broadly speaking, 150-200,000 jobs or so.

48:44.220 --> 48:47.150
But that's a very rough estimate, and, of course,

48:47.150 --> 48:51.280
individual participants may have different views.

48:51.280 --> 48:56.430
Again, that's not a forecast, I've made a hypothesis,

48:56.430 --> 48:59.120
which would imply slower improvement in unemployment.

48:59.120 --> 49:00.780
But the possibility, of course,

49:00.780 --> 49:08.980
exists that this recovery will generate a virtuous circle with greater hiring,

49:08.980 --> 49:13.030
which in turn generates more consumer spending, and greater hiring, and so on.

49:13.030 --> 49:15.130
That remains to be seen, and, of course,

49:15.130 --> 49:21.010
which way that goes is going to be a very important determinant of our response.

49:21.010 --> 49:23.240
PETER COOK.

49:24.930 --> 49:27.610
Mr. Chairman, Peter Cook with Bloomberg Television.

49:27.610 --> 49:28.810
Thank you for the time.

49:28.810 --> 49:31.960
First up, sir, on the projections-in your bid for transparency,

49:31.960 --> 49:35.630
for more openness here at the Fed, would you consider, in the future,

49:35.630 --> 49:39.620
you and your colleagues actually attaching your name to the projections here

49:39.620 --> 49:41.530
so that, again, markets, folks

49:41.530 --> 49:44.130
out in the-watching your actions get a better sense

49:44.130 --> 49:45.800
of exactly what you're thinking in the future,

49:45.800 --> 49:46.840
what your colleagues are thinking?

49:46.840 --> 49:50.660
And separately, sir, as you consider where you are at this moment,

49:50.660 --> 49:53.800
all you've been through with the financial crisis, the recovery,

49:53.800 --> 49:57.120
what is it right now that most frustrates you about this recovery,

49:57.120 --> 50:01.590
that still confounds you most about this recovery, that confuses you most?

50:01.590 --> 50:02.110
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

50:02.110 --> 50:04.000
Well, those things are not all the same.

50:04.000 --> 50:09.960
On the former, our subcommittee, headed by Vice Chair Yellen,

50:09.960 --> 50:14.020
is looking at the SEP for ways to improve transparency,

50:14.020 --> 50:15.610
and we're looking at everything.

50:15.610 --> 50:18.220
So, your particular suggestion is on the table,

50:18.220 --> 50:21.360
as are other ways to try to make this more useful.

50:21.360 --> 50:25.900
And, again, it's a work in progress, as I said before.

50:27.220 --> 50:30.470
The most frustrating aspect, I guess,

50:30.470 --> 50:34.710
of the recovery has been that it's-has been that it's been quite slow.

50:34.710 --> 50:39.380
And as a result, here we are, almost three years from the beginning

50:39.380 --> 50:44.400
of the expansion, and the unemployment rate is still over 8 percent.

50:44.400 --> 50:49.480
So we're looking and hoping that as the headwinds lift,

50:49.480 --> 50:53.830
as financial stresses ease, as housing improves,

50:53.830 --> 50:55.920
that we'll begin to get more rapid improvement

50:55.920 --> 50:58.440
in the economy and in the labor market.

50:58.440 --> 51:03.680
But it has been a very long slog, and that, I think,

51:03.680 --> 51:06.580
would be the single most concerning thing.

51:06.580 --> 51:08.630
From a policy perspective, there are many issues,

51:08.630 --> 51:11.570
like the fiscal issues, for example.

51:11.570 --> 51:13.930
But within the province of the Federal Reserve,

51:13.930 --> 51:26.680
I think it would be the pace of improvement in the labor market.

51:26.680 --> 51:31.170
Thank you.

